From: Luc3as <luc3as@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 17:14:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/25] An enrichment loop: let an outside agent judge a
 listing before it is sent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The app knows what the catalog says. Whether the jacket in the photos is the
model that was searched for, what it costs new, and whether the price is a
bargain or a warning sign are questions for something that can look at
pictures and search the web — an n8n workflow with an LLM, say — and that
should not live inside a polling bot.

So: the webhook destination fires at once and its payload now carries an
"enrichment_url" for each listing (plus every photo URL and the seller's
review count); chat notifications are held for ENRICHMENT_WAIT_S; a verdict
posted to that URL — deal score, identified model, retail price, whether it
matches the search, authenticity risk, one line for a human — is stored on
the listing and releases the held alert immediately, carrying the verdict.
If nothing arrives, the alert goes out as before: the agent's silence costs
a delay, never an alert.

Telegram headlines a hot deal, mutes a dull one (no notification sound below
ENRICHMENT_SILENT_BELOW, or when the agent says it is not the product
searched for), and appends the model and verdict. Discord gets a field, the
dashboard a score badge. The callback accepts the dashboard's token as a
bearer, so a machine can use the same secret a browser does. The loop is
off by default; docs/enrichment.md is the contract for whoever builds the
agent side.
